.TH TXM 1 "October 2026" "txm" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
txm \- A terminal session manager with multi-backend support
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B txm
[\fICOMMAND\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBtxm\fR is a command-line utility for managing terminal multiplexer sessions with support for multiple backends: native, tmux, zellij, and GNU Screen. It automatically detects available backends and provides a unified interface for session management using a concrete noun-verb structure.
.SH BACKEND SUPPORT
\fBtxm\fR supports four terminal multiplexer backends:
.TP
\fBnative\fR
Built-in native persistence using libghostty, implemented by Linux/macOS CGO builds (Linux amd64 runtime validated; macOS unverified); Windows native persistence is unsupported. Framed v1 IPC uses a private per-user runtime directory. Legacy native sockets remain untouched and require the old binary.
.TP
\fBtmux\fR
Exact session/window/pane IDs and names, bounded screen/history capture and read-only attach. Requires a compatible 3.x CLI; local runtime evidence covers 3.4.
.TP
\fBzellij\fR
Degraded session/tab adapter; real runtime unverified. Exact pane, window close/rename/split and output capture are unsupported. Listings may include resurrectable sessions, with unknown process state.
.TP
\fBGNU Screen\fR
Degraded session adapter retaining full PID.name identity. Duplicate short names are ambiguous. Exact pane/window mutations and output capture are unsupported until verified.

Backend selection follows this priority order:
0. Strict \fB\-\-backend\fR or qualified \fBbackend/name\fR target, without fallback.
1. \fBTXM_DEFAULT_BACKEND\fR environment variable
2. User configuration in \fI~/.txm/config\fR
3. Default preference tmux; fallback order tmux, screen, zellij, native. Preferences may fall back when unavailable.
.SH READ OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-backend native|tmux|zellij|screen
Strict selection for reads and mutations. Qualified backend/name targets must agree with this flag; canonical targets do not receive the session prefix again.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-backends
Aggregate session list/capability reads or dashboard scope, attempting all four backends. Conflicts with --backend. Mutations and nuke always remain scoped to one backend.
.TP
.B \-\-json
One version-1 envelope on stdout for session list/inspect/output and backend capabilities, including expected failures. Required fields: schema_version, data, errors, partial. Same-named sessions remain separate; unmanaged sessions have no managed ID or child exit status.
.TP
.B \-\-lines N, \-\-bytes N
Output collection limits: 1..10000 lines (default 100), 1..1048576 bytes (default 1048576). Requests have finite deadlines; truncated output is explicit in JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-plain, \-\-raw
Output defaults to safe plain UTF-8 with terminal controls removed. Raw is explicit opt-in, conflicts with plain, and uses base64 content in JSON. Hidden dump shares the same limits and safe default.
.TP
.B \-\-history
Explicit bounded history, currently tmux-only. Otherwise capture is the visible screen snapshot, not a transcript or command-completion receipt.
.PP
Put flags before positional arguments on create/attach/exec; trailing argv and terminal input, including leading dashes, remain literal.
.SH CONFIGURATION
\fBtxm\fR supports configuration via:
.TP
\fB~/.txm/config\fR
Key=value configuration file for persistent settings. Invalid backend values and unreadable files return errors.
.TP
\fBTXM_DEFAULT_BACKEND\fR
Backend preference (values: native, tmux, zellij, screen), permitting fallback.
.TP
Configuration commands:
.B txm config set backend [BACKEND]
Set or show the default backend.
.B txm config show
Show all configuration.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBsession\fR [\fIcreate|list|inspect|output|attach|detach|delete|rename|nuke\fR]
Manage sessions. (Aliases: \fBcreate\fR, \fBlist\fR, \fBattach\fR, \fBdetach\fR, \fBdelete\fR, \fBrename-session\fR, \fBnuke\fR are available at the root level).
.br
\fBsession create\fR [\fISESSION_NAME\fR] - Create a new session. Use \fB\-\-log\fR to log PTY output.
.br
\fBsession list\fR - List active sessions.
.br
\fBsession inspect\fR TARGET - Inspect one exact session and its capabilities.
.br
\fBsession output\fR TARGET - Capture bounded terminal output.
.br
\fBsession attach\fR [\fISESSION_NAME\fR] - Attach to a session, creating only after proven absence. With \fB\-r\fR, native/tmux enforce read-only attach and never create a missing session; other adapters return unsupported.
.br
\fBsession detach\fR - Detach from the current session.
.br
\fBsession delete\fR [\fISESSION_NAME\fR] - Delete a session.
.br
\fBsession rename\fR [\fIOLD\fR] [\fINEW\fR] - Rename a session.
.br
\fBsession nuke\fR - Remove sessions in the selected backend. Native refuses when incompatible legacy sessions are present.
.TP
\fBbackend capabilities\fR
Report supported/degraded/unsupported capability levels, version and availability. Missing or incompatible backends are errors, including in aggregate results.
.TP
\fBprofile\fR [\fIPATH_TO_TOML\fR]
Strictly validate and apply a tmux 3.4+ or native single-terminal profile. Unknown
fields, invalid cwd/names/splits/layouts and unsupported backend shapes fail before
creation. Profile backend is strict and must agree with --backend. Relative cwd
resolves against the profile file; omitted cwd inherits invocation cwd.
.br
\fBprofile --dry-run\fR PATH shows targets/capabilities/cwd and command presence,
without printing commands, discovering sessions or creating runtime/host state.
.br
\fBprofile --detach\fR PATH applies without attachment. By default, apply attaches.
Existing sessions conflict unless --attach-existing permits unchanged attachment;
--read-only additionally requires that flag and proven existence, never creation.
.br
Limits: 1 MiB, 32 windows, 128 panes. Unnamed tmux windows become window-1, etc.
First panes cannot split; additional splits default vertical from the preceding
returned pane ID. Layout presets: even-horizontal, even-vertical, main-horizontal,
main-vertical, tiled. Native permits one unnamed/0 virtual window and one pane.
.br
Shells launch at the requested cwd using SHELL (default /bin/sh), overriding tmux
default-command. Commands are terminal input, without completion receipts. All
topology is built before input. New panes disable synchronization; ordinary exec
refuses synchronization. Screen/Zellij profiles are unsupported.
.br
Rollback checks exact creation identity and ownership. Uncertain resources remain
intact with partial error 7; cancellation exits 130. Accepted input may have
external effects that rollback cannot undo.
.TP
\fBdashboard\fR [--backend NAME|--all-backends] [--agents] [--preview] [--refresh D]
Interactive local session observations and optional M2 registrations. Separate
from the unchanged bare txm picker. Requires terminal stdin/stdout; non-TTY use
fails with exit 2 before backend/state access. Default all local adapters;
--backend is strict. --refresh defaults to 2s (500ms..1m). Only explicit --agents
starts/subscribes to the optional coordinator. Non-Linux dashboard runtime is unsupported pending platform ancestry/TTY validation.
.PP
Arrow/j/k and page keys navigate; / edits search; Escape clears search/query
before quitting; Tab cycles state/type filters; b cycles backend filters; Enter
attaches and o attaches read-only where verified; p toggles bounded plain preview;
r refreshes; q quits; Ctrl-C cancels (130). Unsupported/stale actions are disabled.
.PP
Session and agent rows are separate, never associated by reusable name. Agent
lifecycle, attention source/time, turn completion and exit receipts stay distinct.
Missing heartbeats become stale/unknown. Native attach/capture pins the instance;
tmux IDs are current-server scoped. Screen/Zellij dashboard actions are disabled.
.PP
Independent two-second reads cannot block keyboard input. At most 2048 session
rows plus 256 registrations are displayed, with explicit truncation. Preview is
opt-in, selected session only, at most 16 KiB/100 visible-screen lines. Controls
are stripped and no output is recorded. Screen storage is capped at 512x200.
TTY attributes/file flags restore on quit, cancellation and attach return.
Inherited platform release gates and provider runtime limits remain in force.
.TP
\fBagent start\fR [\fB--cwd\fR DIR] [\fB--env\fR NAME] NAME -- EXECUTABLE [ARGS...]
Opt in to supervised execution in a new detached native/tmux 3.4+ session. Argv
is executed directly from a private one-use manifest, without a shell. Cwd is
applied before execution. --env permits variable names from the backend wrapper
environment; HOME, PATH, TERM are defaults. Backend environments may predate the
CLI request. Values/argv are never retained in the registry or diagnostics.
.br
Returns an opaque agent ID. Exit 0 means the launch or a durable exit receipt was
observed; inspect receipt.exit_code/outcome to determine the child's result.
Failed exec has start_failed with no invented child exit code. Ambiguous launch
returns partial failure and its ID; do not automatically retry.
.TP
\fBagent register\fR TARGET
Register an existing exact native/tmux session without supervision. Registration
has no managed-session ID, launch identity or retroactive exit receipt.
.TP
\fBagent list\fR / \fBagent status\fR ID
Query/reconcile local agent state. --json uses the version-1 envelope. Status uses
a full opaque ID; --backend filters list and must agree with a status target.
Lifecycle, attention, source and observation time are separate. Attention remains
unknown until verified hooks exist. Missing receipts after wrapper/server loss
mean unknown, never exit 0. Recovery does not rerun commands.
.TP
\fBagent prune\fR ID [\fB--dry-run\fR | \fB--confirm\fR ID]
Remove one exact registration. Supervised agents require a validated exit
receipt; unsupervised pruning leaves the terminal session running. Retain exit
receipts and a bounded replay-prevention ID history. No process is killed.
.TP
\fBtask prune\fR ID [\fB--dry-run\fR | \fB--confirm\fR ID]
Remove one cleaned task record only after its worktree is absent and supervised
exit is proven. Retain Git branches, commits, receipts and agent registration.
Prune the task before its agent. Actual pruning requires the matching full ID.
.TP
\fBagent start --provider\fR generic|claude NAME -- EXECUTABLE [ARGS...]
Explicitly provide private hook context to a managed child. Claude receives a
private generated settings file; user/project settings are not edited. Only exact
verified provider versions are accepted. Generic operation needs no provider.
.TP
\fBagent hooks enable\fR ID [--provider generic|claude|codex]
Create or rotate a mode-0600 per-instance/generation credential. Same UID is the
trust boundary. Tokens never enter argv, environment, public status or events.
.TP
\fBagent hooks config\fR ID / \fBagent providers\fR [--json]
Print reviewable provider settings or installed adapter availability. Codex
requires manual provider trust review. OpenCode v2 remains unsupported. No
permission bypass or automatic approval is provided.
.TP
\fBagent signal\fR ID --instance I --generation G --sequence N --time RFC3339 --state STATE
Report authenticated unknown, working, needs-input, idle or turn-complete. A
managed child may instead use --from-env with --state. Sequences must increase;
timestamps must be fresh. Attention expires after two minutes. Signals cannot
establish process exit. Turn completion is separate and clears on new work.
.TP
\fBevents --jsonl\fR [--id ID] [--timeout D]
Stream safe version-1 epoch/sequence events. Initial resync_required includes an
atomic current snapshot; overflow/reconnect resnapshots. Eight subscribers,
16-event queues, bounded writes, no durable replay or exactly-once guarantee.
No prompts, transcripts or terminal contents are included.
.TP
\fBagent wait\fR ID [--until exited|needs-input|idle] [--timeout D] [--fail-on-nonzero]
Wait using atomic snapshot/subscription. Default 30s, maximum 24h. Exit 0 means
observed condition; a nonzero child exit also satisfies exited. With
--fail-on-nonzero an unsuccessful receipt returns 1. Timeout 6, cancellation 130,
unsupported 4, unproven loss 1. JSON retains the last observation on failure.
.TP
\fBagent notify\fR -- EXECUTABLE [ARGS...]
Explicitly run an action for future needs-input, turn-complete or exit events.
Safe JSON metadata arrives on stdin. Two-second deadline, five-second per-agent
throttle, bounded local queue; snapshot/resync never replays actions. Default off.
.TP
\fBagent stop-coordinator\fR
Stop only the matching local agent service, leaving managed sessions/children
running. Only agent workflows/events start/contact the optional service; ordinary session
commands remain independent. Private versioned registry/manifest/receipt files
are under the state directory. Phase 5 runtime evidence is Linux amd64;
M1 cross-platform release gates and later M2 hooks/events/wait remain pending.
.TP
\fBwindow\fR [\fInew|list|kill|next|prev|rename|split\fR]
Manage windows within a session.
.br
\fBwindow new\fR [\fISESSION_NAME\fR] [\fIWINDOW_NAME\fR] - Create a new window.
.br
\fBwindow split\fR [\fISESSION_NAME\fR] [\fIWINDOW_NAME\fR] [\fIv|h\fR] - Split a window.
.TP
\fBpane\fR [\fIlist|kill|exec\fR]
Manage panes within a window.
.br
\fBpane exec\fR [\fISESSION_NAME\fR] [\fIWINDOW_NAME\fR] [\fIPANE_NUMBER\fR] [\fICOMMAND\fR] - Send terminal input without a completion/exit-status promise. Native accepts window 0 and pane 0 only. (Alias: \fBexec\fR is available at the root level).
.TP
\fBcompletion\fR [\fIbash|zsh|fish|powershell\fR] [\fB\-\-install\fR]
Generate the autocompletion script. Use \fB\-\-install\fR to configure RC files automatically.
.TP
\fBgenerate-ssh-config\fR
Generate an SSH config snippet for seamless remote workflows.
.TP
\fBinstall\fR
Install txm binary and man page.
.TP
\fBuninstall\fR
Uninstall txm cleanly from your system.
.TP
\fBupdate\fR
Update txm to the latest version directly from GitHub Releases.
.TP
\fBversion\fR [\fB\-\-check-update\fR]
Show version information.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Enable verbose output for debugging.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TXM_DEFAULT_BACKEND
Set the default backend to use (native, tmux, zellij, or screen). Overrides configuration file settings.
.TP
.B TXM_SESSION_PREFIX
Automatically namespaces session names across all commands.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set, disables colored output.
.TP
.B TERM
Used to detect terminal capabilities for colored output.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Create a new session:
.B txm session create mysession
.TP
List all sessions:
.B txm list
.TP
Set default backend to zellij:
.B txm config set backend zellij
.TP
Use specific backend via environment:
.B TXM_DEFAULT_BACKEND=tmux txm create mysession
.TP
Create and split a window:
.B txm window new mysession mywindow
.B txm window split mysession mywindow v
.TP
Execute a command in a pane:
.B txm pane exec mysession mywindow 0 "ls -la"
.SH OPTIONAL EXTENSIONS
.TP
.B task start NAME --repo DIR [--base REV] [--branch NEW] [--dry-run] [--allow-repo-commands] -- EXECUTABLE [ARGS...]
Create a fresh Git branch and private worktree with a managed agent ID. Strict native default or explicit tmux. Dry-run redacts command/environment values and creates no task state. Hooks and external filters require opt-in; no setup script is inferred.
.TP
.B task list | inspect ID | stop ID | cleanup ID
Actions require full task IDs. Cleanup requires an exit receipt or an authoritative orphan, verifies filesystem ownership, and refuses modified/untracked/ignored files. Force requires --force --confirm ID and never bypasses process or ownership proof. Branches/commits remain. Interrupted starts are inspectable; no automatic launch replay or rollback.
.TP
.B remote allow ALIAS [--host-id ID] [--executable PATH] | hosts | probe ALIAS | pin ALIAS ID | forget ALIAS
Explicit private SSH alias allowlist and host pins. Reuses OpenSSH configuration/authentication with batch mode and strict host keys. Probe negotiates schema/protocol 1; unpinned hosts cannot list/attach.
.TP
.B remote list [ALIAS...] [--timeout DURATION]
List allowed pinned hosts using native (default) or explicit tmux. Per-host bounds are 1..30 seconds with concurrency four; reports alias-scoped partial failures. No network service is exposed.
.TP
.B remote attach ALIAS --host-id ID --backend BACKEND --name NAME --session-id ID [--instance ID] [--read-only]
TTY-only attachment to an exact observed native/tmux session. Native requires its exact instance. Requests use encoded JSON tokens; local TTY settings/flags are restored. JSON attachment is unsupported.
.PP
Extensions have Linux development fixture coverage. Actual SSH server/network and inherited platform release gates remain unrun. See docs/extensions.md for recovery and limits.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/txm/v1/host.json
Private random local host ID, otherwise ~/.local/state/txm/v1/host.json. Structured-state reads initialize it atomically; corrupt/unsafe files are errors. Picker previews and ordinary mutations do not initialize this state or start an agent coordinator. Private structured state is unsupported on unverified platforms.
.TP
.I ~/.txm/config
Configuration file (if supported).
.TP
.I ~/.local/bin/txm
Default user installation location.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/txm
System-wide installation location.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 success/empty; 1 general/config failure; 2 invalid usage/target; 3 not found;
4 unsupported; 5 conflict/ambiguous; 6 timeout; 7 unavailable/partial; 8 denied;
130 cancelled. Partial aggregate data exits 7 unless interrupted (130). JSON
errors include code, message, backend/locator when applicable, and retryable.
.PP
Linux amd64 is locally validated. macOS runtime and actual release-archive
verification remain required gates; Windows native persistence is unsupported.
